IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wra-core`
IMPORT `wra4-s105-recovery`

-- Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5, Part 5, ss 113 to 116: penalties as an alternative to prosecution
-- (SSAA 115A, 115B) and civil penalties (SSAA 115C, 115D). Layer 1, part wra4.
-- Host Act named in labels: SSAA (Social Security Administration Act 1992). It is not among the sources: what it says
-- elsewhere (for example 115A(8), 121DA(7)) is a borrowed fact, asked as an input.
-- Result convention: JUST FALSE / FALSE = the text says no; NOTHING = not covered (see wra4-ss96-97.l4).

§ `s 113 -- penalty for benefit fraud not resulting in overpayment`

-- Inserted SSAA 115A(1A): the penalty provision "also applies" where (a) there are grounds for proceedings for an offence
-- relating to an act or omission in relation to any benefit and (b) an overpayment, had one been made, would have been
-- recoverable under section 71, 71ZB, 71A, 75 or 76.
DECLARE `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) provision under which the overpayment would have been recoverable` IS ONE OF
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 71`
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 71ZB`
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 71A`
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 75`
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 76`
    `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) some other provision`

-- src:1889 | (1A) This section also applies where —
-- src:1890 | (a) it appears to the Secretary of State or an authority that there are grounds for instituting proceedings against a person for an offence (under this Act or any other enactment) relating to an act or omission on the part of that person in relation to any benefit, and
-- src:1891 | (b) if an overpayment attributable to the act or omission had been made, the overpayment would have been recoverable from the person by, or due from the person to, the Secretary of State or an authority under or by virtue of section 71, 71ZB, 71A, 75 or 76 above.
@ref s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A) (src:1889-1891)
GIVEN `it appears that there are grounds for instituting proceedings for an offence relating to an act or omission in relation to any benefit` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Does it appear to the Secretary of State or an authority that there are grounds for proceedings against you for an offence about an act or omission of yours in relation to a benefit?
      `where an overpayment, had one been made, would have been recoverable` IS A `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) provision under which the overpayment would have been recoverable`  @desc If you had been overpaid because of that act or omission, under which section of the Administration Act would the overpayment have been recoverable (71, 71ZB, 71A, 75 or 76), or some other provision?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A) the penalty provision also applies` MEANS
    IF `it appears that there are grounds for instituting proceedings for an offence relating to an act or omission in relation to any benefit`
    THEN (CONSIDER `where an overpayment, had one been made, would have been recoverable`
          WHEN `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 71` THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 71ZB` THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 71A` THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 75` THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) section 76` THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN `s 113(2) SSAA 115A(1A)(b) some other provision` THEN NOTHING)  -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): subsection (1A) is "also" a ground for applying the section; it does not say it never applies otherwise
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "This section also applies where" (a) and (b)

§ `s 114 -- amount of penalty`

-- Substituted SSAA 115A(3): the penalty is 50% of the overpayment (rounded down to the nearest whole penny), subject to a
-- minimum of 350 pounds and a maximum of 2000 pounds. The Secretary of State may amend these figures by order (115A(3B)),
-- so a later order would change these answers. The rounding is read as applying to the 50% (fork register).
-- src:1904 | (3) The amount of the penalty in a case falling within subsection (1) is 50% of the amount of the overpayment (rounded down to the nearest whole penny), subject to—
-- src:1905 | (a) a minimum amount of £350, and
-- src:1906 | (b) a maximum amount of £2000.
@ref s 114(1) SSAA 115A(3) (src:1904-1906)
GIVEN `the amount of the overpayment, in pounds` IS A NUMBER  @desc How much were you overpaid, in pounds?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 114(1) SSAA 115A(3) the amount of the penalty` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `half the overpayment, rounded down to the nearest whole penny` LESS THAN 350
        THEN 350                                          -- the minimum amount of 350 pounds
        IF `half the overpayment, rounded down to the nearest whole penny` GREATER THAN 2000
        THEN 2000                                         -- the maximum amount of 2000 pounds
        OTHERWISE `half the overpayment, rounded down to the nearest whole penny`
    WHERE
        `half the overpayment, rounded down to the nearest whole penny` MEANS
            (FLOOR ((`the amount of the overpayment, in pounds` TIMES 100) DIVIDED BY 2)) DIVIDED BY 100

-- Substituted SSAA 115A(3A): in a case within subsection (1A) the amount of the penalty is 350 pounds.
-- src:1907 | (3A) The amount of the penalty in a case falling within subsection (1A) is £350.
@ref s 114(1) SSAA 115A(3A) (src:1907-1907)
`s 114(1) SSAA 115A(3A) the amount of the penalty in a case within subsection (1A)` MEANS 350

§ `s 115 -- period for withdrawal of agreement to pay penalty`

-- s 115(2), (3): the cooling-off period in SSAA 115A(5) and 115B(6) is changed from "28" to "14". The text shows the numbers
-- only: neither the unit nor the day the period starts is printed here (fork register). The unit is taken to be days and the
-- count to begin with the day of the agreement; a withdrawal after the 14th day is not covered.
-- src:1915 | (2) In section 115A (penalty as alternative to prosecution), in subsection (5) (cooling-off period), for “28” there is substituted “ 14 ”.
@ref s 115(2) SSAA 115A(5) (src:1915-1915)
GIVEN `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` IS A NUMBER  @desc How many days after you agreed to pay the penalty did you withdraw your agreement (0 if the same day)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 115(2) SSAA 115A(5) the agreement is withdrawn within the cooling-off period` MEANS
    IF `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` AT LEAST 0
       AND `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` AT MOST 14
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the amended subsection (5) is not printed, so the text does not say what follows a later withdrawal, nor where the 14 are counted from

-- src:1916 | (3) In section 115B (penalty as alternative to prosecution: colluding employers etc), in subsection (6) (cooling-off period), for “28” there is substituted “ 14 ”.
@ref s 115(3) SSAA 115B(6) (src:1916-1916)
GIVEN `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` IS A NUMBER  @desc How many days after you agreed to pay the penalty did you withdraw your agreement (0 if the same day)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 115(3) SSAA 115B(6) the agreement is withdrawn within the cooling-off period` MEANS
    IF `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` AT LEAST 0
       AND `the number of days from the agreement to pay the penalty until its withdrawal` AT MOST 14
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the amended subsection (6) is not printed, so the text does not say what follows a later withdrawal, nor where the 14 are counted from

§ `s 116 -- civil penalties (SSAA 115C and 115D)`

-- 115C(6) "appropriate authority" means the Secretary of State or an authority which administers housing benefit or council
-- tax benefit. A closed definition ("means").
-- src:1936 | “appropriate authority” means—
-- src:1937 | the Secretary of State, or
-- src:1938 | an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit;
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(6) “appropriate authority” (src:1936-1938)
GIVEN `the body` IS AN Authority
      `the body administers housing benefit or council tax benefit` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Does the body administer housing benefit or council tax benefit?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(6) the body is the appropriate authority` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the body`
    WHEN `the Secretary of State` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a local authority` THEN `the body administers housing benefit or council tax benefit`  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means— ... an authority which administers housing benefit or council tax benefit" (it does not)
    WHEN `an authorised person` THEN FALSE                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)
    WHEN `an employment officer` THEN FALSE               -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)
    WHEN `Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)
    WHEN `the First-tier Tribunal` THEN FALSE             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)
    WHEN `the Upper Tribunal` THEN FALSE                  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)
    WHEN `a court` THEN FALSE                             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)
    WHEN `the Director of Public Prosecutions` THEN FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate authority” means—" (closed list)

-- 115C(6) "overpayment" and "relevant social security benefit" have the meanings given in 115A(8) and 121DA(7), which are
-- not among the sources: asked as facts.
-- src:1939 | “overpayment” has the meaning given in section 115A(8), and the reference to the making of an overpayment is to be construed in accordance with that provision;
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(6) “overpayment” (src:1939-1939)
GIVEN `the payment is an overpayment within the meaning of SSAA section 115A(8)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the payment an overpayment in the sense of section 115A(8) of the Administration Act? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(6) the payment is an overpayment` MEANS
    `the payment is an overpayment within the meaning of SSAA section 115A(8)`  -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "has the meaning given in section 115A(8)", which is not among the sources

-- src:1940 | “relevant social security benefit” has the meaning given in section 121DA(7).
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(6) “relevant social security benefit” (src:1940-1940)
GIVEN `the benefit is a relevant social security benefit within the meaning of SSAA section 121DA(7)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the benefit a "relevant social security benefit" in the sense of section 121DA(7) of the Administration Act? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(6) the benefit is a relevant social security benefit` MEANS
    `the benefit is a relevant social security benefit within the meaning of SSAA section 121DA(7)`  -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "has the meaning given in section 121DA(7)", which is not among the sources

-- 115C(1): the section applies where (a) the person negligently makes an incorrect statement or gives incorrect information in
-- or in connection with a claim for, or in connection with an award of, a relevant social security benefit, (b) fails to take
-- reasonable steps to correct the error, (c) the error results in an overpayment, and (d) the person has not been charged,
-- cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment.
-- src:1922 | (1) This section applies where—
-- src:1923 | (a) a person negligently makes an incorrect statement or representation, or negligently gives incorrect information or evidence—
-- src:1924 | (i) in or in connection with a claim for a relevant social security benefit, or
-- src:1925 | (ii) in connection with an award of a relevant social security benefit,
-- src:1926 | (b) the person fails to take reasonable steps to correct the error,
-- src:1927 | (c) the error results in the making of an overpayment, and
-- src:1928 | (d) the person has not been charged with an offence or cautioned, or been given a notice under section 115A, in respect of the overpayment.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(1) (src:1922-1928)
GIVEN `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in or in connection with a claim` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you negligently make an incorrect statement, or give incorrect information or evidence, in or in connection with a claim for the benefit?
      `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in connection with an award` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you negligently make an incorrect statement, or give incorrect information or evidence, in connection with an award of the benefit?
      `the person failed to take reasonable steps to correct the error` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you fail to take reasonable steps to correct the error?
      `the error resulted in the making of an overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did the error result in your being overpaid?
      `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you been charged with an offence, cautioned, or given a penalty notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(1) the section applies` MEANS
    IF (`the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in or in connection with a claim`
          OR `the person negligently made an incorrect statement or gave incorrect information in connection with an award`)
       AND `the person failed to take reasonable steps to correct the error`
       AND `the error resulted in the making of an overpayment`
       AND (NOT (`the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "This section applies where" (a) to (d)

-- 115C(2)(a): a penalty of a prescribed amount may be imposed by the appropriate authority on the person.
-- src:1929 | (2) A penalty of a prescribed amount may be imposed by the appropriate authority—
-- src:1930 | (a) in any case, on the person;
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(2)(a) (src:1929-1930)
GIVEN `section 115C(1) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(2)(a) a penalty may be imposed on the person` MEANS
    IF `section 115C(1) applies` THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the penalty power arises under "this section", which applies "where" 115C(1) is met

-- 115C(2)(b) and (3): in a joint claim by A and B, the penalty may be imposed on B instead of A; but (2)(b) "does not apply if"
-- B was not, and could not reasonably be expected to have been, aware that A made the incorrect statement.
-- src:1931 | (b) in a case where the person (“A”) is making, or has made, a claim for the benefit for a period jointly with another (“B”), on B instead of A.
-- src:1932 | (3) Subsection (2)(b) does not apply if B was not, and could not reasonably be expected to have been, aware that A had negligently made the incorrect statement or representation or given the incorrect information or evidence.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(2)(b) and (3) (src:1931-1932)
GIVEN `section 115C(1) applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claim is made jointly with another` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you making, or have you made, the claim jointly with another person?
      `the other joint claimant was aware, or could reasonably be expected to have been aware, of the incorrect statement` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Were you aware, or could you reasonably be expected to have been aware, that the other joint claimant had made the incorrect statement or given the incorrect information?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(2)(b) a penalty may be imposed on the other joint claimant instead` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`section 115C(1) applies`))
        THEN NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the penalty power arises only where 115C(1) is met
        IF (NOT (`the claim is made jointly with another`))
        THEN NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): paragraph (b) is "in a case where the person (“A”) is making ... a claim ... jointly with another (“B”)"
        IF `the other joint claimant was aware, or could reasonably be expected to have been aware, of the incorrect statement`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE                              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (2)(b) does not apply if B was not, and could not reasonably be expected to have been, aware"

-- 115C(4), 115D(4): a penalty imposed is recoverable by the appropriate authority from the person on whom it is imposed.
-- src:1933 | (4) A penalty imposed under subsection (2) is recoverable by the appropriate authority from the person on whom it is imposed.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(4) (src:1933-1933)
GIVEN `the penalty was imposed on the person under subsection (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(4) the penalty is recoverable from the person` MEANS
    IF `the penalty was imposed on the person under subsection (2)` THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "A penalty imposed under subsection (2) is recoverable ... from the person on whom it is imposed"

-- 115C(5), 115D(5): 71ZC, 71ZD and 71ZE apply to the penalty as to 71ZB amounts (not 71ZF): the same ways as 115B(4A).
-- src:1934 | (5) Sections 71ZC, 71ZD and 71ZE apply in relation to amounts recoverable by the appropriate authority under subsection (4) as to amounts recoverable by the Secretary of State under section 71ZB (and, where the appropriate authority is not the Secretary of State, those sections so apply as if references to the Secretary of State were to that authority).
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115C(5) (src:1934-1934)
GIVEN `the way` IS A `Way of recovering an amount`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115C(5) the penalty may be recovered in this way` MEANS
    `s 105(4) SSAA 115B(4A) the penalty may be recovered in this way` `the way`

-- 115D(1): a penalty of a prescribed amount may be imposed where the person, without reasonable excuse, fails to provide
-- information or evidence as the appropriate authority requires in connection with a claim or award, the failure results in an
-- overpayment, and the person has not been charged, cautioned or given a notice under 115A in respect of it.
-- src:1942 | (1) A penalty of a prescribed amount may be imposed on a person by the appropriate authority where—
-- src:1943 | (a) the person, without reasonable excuse, fails to provide information or evidence in accordance with requirements imposed on the person by the appropriate authority in connection with a claim for, or an award of, a relevant social security benefit,
-- src:1944 | (b) the failure results in the making of an overpayment, and
-- src:1945 | (c) the person has not been charged with an offence or cautioned, or been given a notice under section 115A, in respect of the overpayment.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115D(1) (src:1942-1945)
GIVEN `the person failed to provide information or evidence as the appropriate authority required` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you fail to provide information or evidence that the authority required of you in connection with a claim for, or an award of, a benefit?
      `the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse for the failure?
      `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did the failure result in your being overpaid?
      `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you been charged with an offence, cautioned, or given a penalty notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115D(1) a penalty may be imposed for failing to provide information` MEANS
    IF `the person failed to provide information or evidence as the appropriate authority required`
       AND (NOT (`the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure`))
       AND `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment`
       AND (NOT (`the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the power arises "where" (a), (b) and (c) are met

-- 115D(2): the same where the person, without reasonable excuse, fails to notify a relevant change of circumstances.
-- src:1946 | (2) A penalty of a prescribed amount may be imposed on a person by the appropriate authority where—
-- src:1947 | (a) the person, without reasonable excuse, fails to notify the appropriate authority of a relevant change of circumstances in accordance with requirements imposed on the person under relevant social security legislation,
-- src:1948 | (b) the failure results in the making of an overpayment, and
-- src:1949 | (c) the person has not been charged with an offence or cautioned, or been given a notice under section 115A, in respect of the overpayment.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115D(2) (src:1946-1949)
GIVEN `the person failed to notify a relevant change of circumstances as required` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you fail to tell the authority about a change of circumstances that you were required to notify?
      `the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did you have a reasonable excuse for the failure?
      `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did the failure result in your being overpaid?
      `the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Have you been charged with an offence, cautioned, or given a penalty notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115D(2) a penalty may be imposed for failing to notify a change of circumstances` MEANS
    IF `the person failed to notify a relevant change of circumstances as required`
       AND (NOT (`the person had a reasonable excuse for the failure`))
       AND `the failure resulted in the making of an overpayment`
       AND (NOT (`the person has been charged, cautioned or given a notice under section 115A in respect of the overpayment`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the power arises "where" (a), (b) and (c) are met

-- 115D(3): joint claimants who both fail: "only one penalty may be imposed in respect of the same overpayment".
-- src:1950 | (3) Where a person is making, or has made, a claim for a benefit for a period jointly with another, and both of them fail as mentioned in subsection (1) or (2), only one penalty may be imposed in respect of the same overpayment.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115D(3) (src:1950-1950)
GIVEN `the claim is made jointly with another` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you making, or have you made, the claim jointly with another person?
      `a penalty has already been imposed on the other joint claimant for the same overpayment` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has a penalty already been imposed on the other joint claimant for the same overpayment?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115D(3) a further penalty may be imposed for the same overpayment` MEANS
    IF `the claim is made jointly with another` AND `a penalty has already been imposed on the other joint claimant for the same overpayment`
    THEN JUST FALSE                                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "only one penalty may be imposed in respect of the same overpayment"
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the bar is for joint claimants "both" failing, in respect of "the same overpayment"

-- src:1951 | (4) A penalty imposed under subsection (1) or (2) is recoverable by the appropriate authority from the person on whom it is imposed.
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115D(4) (src:1951-1951)
GIVEN `the penalty was imposed on the person under subsection (1) or (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 116(1) SSAA 115D(4) the penalty is recoverable from the person` MEANS
    IF `the penalty was imposed on the person under subsection (1) or (2)` THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "A penalty imposed under subsection (1) or (2) is recoverable ... from the person on whom it is imposed"

-- src:1952 | (5) Sections 71ZC, 71ZD and 71ZE apply in relation to amounts recoverable by the appropriate authority under subsection (4) as to amounts recoverable by the Secretary of State under section 71ZB (and, where the appropriate authority is not the Secretary of State, those sections so apply as if references to the Secretary of State were to that authority).
@ref s 116(1) SSAA 115D(5) (src:1952-1952)
